"A food caterer was told to prepare gourmet hot dogs for 36 guests at a local community event. While most people would only eat one hotdog, he prepared enough for half of the guests to be able to have two hotdogs. However, unexpectedly, 40 guests showed up, and everyone wanted a second hotdog. How many guests did not get a second hotdog?","A food caterer was told to prepare gourmet hot dogs for 36 guests. While most people would only eat one hotdog, he prepared enough for half of the guests to be able to have two hotdogs. However, 40 guests showed up, and everyone wanted a second hotdog. How many guests did not get a second hotdog?",26,26,26,26,"Let’s define what was prepared and what was needed.

- Initial expected guests: 36
- He prepared enough for half of them to have two hot dogs.
  - That means: 36 guests would each get 1 hot dog = 36
  - Plus extra for half (18 guests) to have a second = 18
  - Total hot dogs prepared = 36 + 18 = 54

- Actual guests: 40
- Everyone wanted a second hot dog, so total hot dogs needed = 40 × 2 = 80

- Shortfall in second hot dogs:
  - First, 40 hot dogs go to give everyone their first.
  - Remaining hot dogs for seconds = 54 − 40 = 14
  - Guests wanting a second = 40
  - Guests who did not get a second = 40 − 14 = 26

#### 26",,"Let’s define what was prepared and what was needed.
